Most renewable energy sources, such as wind and solar power, cannot continuously supply energy that is sufficient for the immense energy demands of human societies. Addressing this challenge will require advancements in technology, increased focus in sustainability, and robust grid storage solutions to ensure consistent energy delivery @tang_2019_issues.

Of the current rechargeable battery technologies, the one that would be used for this energy storage would be the lithium-ion battery (LIB). However, lithium-ion batteries are expensive, flammable, and can be quite toxic. Furthermore, lithium supply is limited and not distributed equally around the world. Thus, certain countries may have political motives to control world lithium production, to the harm of others. Additionally, the unsustainable mining efforts required to mine lithium challenge the viability of lithium-ion batteries to help with the transition to renewable energy sources. Nickel-metal hydride (NiMH) batteries will not perform well in this role either due to the long time period over which the battery must store energy, as NiMH batteries lose considerable amounts of energy even while unused in storage (known as "self-discharge"). This effect renders NiMH batteries ineffective and inefficient for usage in grid storage technology @silberberg_2012_chemistry.

In contrast, zinc-ion batteries (ZIBs) offer several advantages over the common lithium-ion batteries for grid storage. ZIBs are much less toxic, non-flammable, and can become much cheaper to produce. Zinc is over ten times as abundant as lithium and far easier to obtain @vannoorden_2014_the. Zinc is widely used in alkaline batteries due to its stability in non-flammable aqueous solutions, low cost, and large electrochemical potential.

== Engineering Goal
This project was conducted to analyze and evaluate the feasibility of zinc-ion batteries using simplified designs and consumer-grade materials to evaluate the long-term viability of these batteries. To do so, the composition of the cathode and the electrolyte were varied to produce the results obtained in this paper. The different battery designs used in this experiment were intentionally left far simpler than those that would be used in building a similar battery at an industrial scale. This allows for a simpler investigation into the workings of the battery and simplifies the evaluation of battery performance.

== History
Alessandro Volta created his first preliminary “battery” from the findings of fellow scientist Luigi Galvani in 1791. Volta's first effective battery, known as the voltaic pile, was invented in 1799 and detailed in a letter he sent to Sir Joseph Banks of The Royal Society of London @volta_1800_on. Pairs of copper and zinc discs were stacked on top of each other, each separated with brine-soaked cloth. While this battery was not very useful, it was the first demonstration of using chemical reactions to produce an electrical potential and led the way for other major inventions in battery technology. The dominant battery types used today include the lead-acid battery, the alkaline battery, the lithium-ion battery, and the nickel-metal hydride battery. In particular, the lithium-ion battery is one of the most important components for the development of modern electronics @zhang_2020_materials.
